1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,984
CART is the second-biggest topic on these midterms.

2
00:00:03,984 --> 00:00:08,231
Every question comes down to a few ideas: how a split is chosen,

3
00:00:08,231 --> 00:00:12,611
how to read a tree, what cp does, and how costs change the leaves.

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:15,952
CART grows a tree greedily.

5
00:00:15,952 --> 00:00:19,716
At each node, it tries every variable and every cut point,

6
00:00:19,716 --> 00:00:24,777
and keeps the single cut that lowers impurity the most: Gini or error rate for

7
00:00:24,777 --> 00:00:28,605
classification, the residual sum of squares for regression.

8
00:00:28,605 --> 00:00:30,552
Then it repeats on each child.

9
00:00:30,552 --> 00:00:35,029
Afterward, it prunes any split that improved the fit by less than cp.

10
00:00:35,787 --> 00:00:41,197
Practice midterm three asks why AverageScore was the only variable used in the golf

11
00:00:41,197 --> 00:00:41,523
tree.

12
00:00:41,523 --> 00:00:44,326
The precise answer: at each of those nodes,

13
00:00:44,326 --> 00:00:49,280
its best cut lowered the RSS more than any cut on the other three variables.

14
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:52,605
The intuition: it's by far the strongest predictor,

15
00:00:52,605 --> 00:00:55,669
with a correlation of minus 0.76 with winnings.

16
00:00:55,669 --> 00:00:59,710
The rubric takes points off if you skip how CART picks splits.

17
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:06,227
To score a cut, CART uses an impurity function of the class mix in a node.

18
00:01:06,227 --> 00:01:11,420
A good one is zero for a pure node, highest at a fifty-fifty mix, and concave,

19
00:01:11,420 --> 00:01:13,550
shaped like an upside-down bowl.

20
00:01:13,550 --> 00:01:15,747
Gini and error rate both qualify.

21
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:17,293
Why concave?

22
00:01:17,293 --> 00:01:21,223
A parent's mix is a weighted average of its children's mixes,

23
00:01:21,223 --> 00:01:26,120
and for a concave function, the children's average impurity can't exceed the

24
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:26,699
parent's.

25
00:01:26,699 --> 00:01:29,405
So a split never increases total impurity.

26
00:01:29,405 --> 00:01:33,980
On practice midterm two, question thirteen, the tent and the dome work,

27
00:01:33,980 --> 00:01:36,170
but the dip and the U-shape don't.

28
00:01:36,170 --> 00:01:37,910
The answer is only B and C.

29
00:01:38,687 --> 00:01:42,330
But never increasing isn't the same as always decreasing.

30
00:01:42,330 --> 00:01:44,695
A split can leave impurity unchanged,

31
00:01:44,695 --> 00:01:49,872
for example under error rate when both children keep the parent's majority class.

32
00:01:49,872 --> 00:01:54,538
So the claim that impurity strictly decreases after every split is false.

33
00:01:56,133 --> 00:02:00,178
Reading a tree: at each node, go left when the condition is true.

34
00:02:00,178 --> 00:02:05,219
Here's the Lending Club tree from practice midterm two, where 1 means a bad risk.

35
00:02:05,987 --> 00:02:10,502
Part b: FICO is 650, and the number of inquiries is unknown.

36
00:02:10,502 --> 00:02:12,760
650 is below 740, so go right.

37
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:17,577
If inquiries are under 3, we check whether FICO is at least 665.

38
00:02:17,577 --> 00:02:19,233
It isn't, so bad risk.

39
00:02:19,233 --> 00:02:22,394
If inquiries are 3 or more, also bad risk.

40
00:02:22,394 --> 00:02:27,737
Every branch you can't rule out ends in bad risk, so that's the answer.

41
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:30,924
Part c: 4 inquiries, FICO unknown.

42
00:02:30,924 --> 00:02:34,388
If FICO is at least 740, the tree says good risk.

43
00:02:34,388 --> 00:02:38,417
If not, 4 inquiries fails the under-3 check, so bad risk.

44
00:02:38,417 --> 00:02:43,012
The branches disagree, so the answer is: you need the FICO score,

45
00:02:43,012 --> 00:02:45,345
and here's what each value gives.

46
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:50,635
One more reading tip from midterm one: on a zero-one variable,

47
00:02:50,635 --> 00:02:53,621
X less than 0.5 just means X equals zero.

48
00:02:53,621 --> 00:02:57,990
That tree predicts a cancellation only for vehicle model 12,

49
00:02:57,990 --> 00:02:59,884
booked on the mobile site.

50
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:03,503
Regression trees predict a number.

51
00:03:03,503 --> 00:03:06,684
Each leaf predicts the average of its training points.

52
00:03:06,684 --> 00:03:09,570
Here's the golf tree from practice midterm three,

53
00:03:09,570 --> 00:03:11,809
where every split is on average score.

54
00:03:12,587 --> 00:03:17,877
So the prediction, as a function of average score,

55
00:03:17,877 --> 00:03:24,754
is a step function: flat inside each region, jumping at each cut.

56
00:03:24,754 --> 00:03:28,880
Below 70.02, it predicts 5.145 million.

57
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:34,170
Then 2.897, then 1.506, and from 71.12 up, 0.8197.

58
00:03:34,953 --> 00:03:36,891
The rubric checks the details.

59
00:03:36,891 --> 00:03:40,766
Label both axes, and draw flat steps, not a continuous line.

60
00:03:40,766 --> 00:03:43,672
Because the splits use greater than or equal,

61
00:03:43,672 --> 00:03:48,838
each cut value belongs to the step on its right: a filled dot at the left end of

62
00:03:48,838 --> 00:03:51,679
each step, and an open dot at its right end.

63
00:03:53,267 --> 00:03:55,549
Now cp, the complexity parameter.

64
00:03:55,549 --> 00:04:01,222
In the pruning phase, a split survives only if it improves the fit by at least cp.

65
00:04:01,222 --> 00:04:06,479
For a regression tree, that means raising training R squared by at least cp.

66
00:04:06,479 --> 00:04:12,082
A bigger cp gives a smaller tree, and a cp of zero keeps everything and overfits.

67
00:04:12,853 --> 00:04:18,800
Practice midterm three: the tree has a training R squared of 0.704,

68
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:20,753
with cp equal to 0.01.

69
00:04:20,753 --> 00:04:23,327
Add one more split to a leaf.

70
00:04:23,327 --> 00:04:28,830
Splits never lower training R squared, so it's at least 0.704.

71
00:04:28,830 --> 00:04:35,399
And if the new split had added more than 0.01, pruning would have kept it.

72
00:04:35,399 --> 00:04:41,257
So the most definitive answer is the interval from 0.704 to 0.714.

73
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:45,557
Now remove the bottom right split instead.

74
00:04:45,557 --> 00:04:50,358
That split was kept, so it must have added at least 0.01.

75
00:04:50,358 --> 00:04:54,149
Removing it drops R squared to at most 0.694.

76
00:04:54,149 --> 00:04:57,770
The answer is the interval from 0 to 0.694.

77
00:04:59,367 --> 00:05:04,313
Practice midterm one tuned cp with ten-fold cross-validation,

78
00:05:04,313 --> 00:05:06,583
using losses of 11.35 and 1.

79
00:05:06,583 --> 00:05:12,988
The output reports both the average loss and the accuracy for each value of cp.

80
00:05:13,753 --> 00:05:18,226
By average loss, the lowest value, 0.3617,

81
00:05:18,226 --> 00:05:22,805
is tied from cp 0.001 all the way to 0.011.

82
00:05:22,805 --> 00:05:30,686
When there's a tie, pick the largest cp, since it gives the simplest tree.

83
00:05:30,686 --> 00:05:32,283
So cp is 0.011.

84
00:05:33,053 --> 00:05:36,847
By accuracy, the best is 0.9606, at cp 0.040.

85
00:05:36,847 --> 00:05:42,075
But with imbalanced classes and unequal costs, trust the loss.

86
00:05:42,075 --> 00:05:48,820
Chasing accuracy drifts toward the baseline: a tree that almost never predicts a

87
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:49,916
cancellation.

88
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:54,964
Costs also change what a leaf predicts.

89
00:05:54,964 --> 00:06:02,248
With losses L_FN and L_FP, a leaf predicts positive when its share of positives is

90
00:06:02,248 --> 00:06:08,289
above L_FP over L_FP plus L_FN, the same p* as in decision analysis.

91
00:06:08,289 --> 00:06:12,464
With losses of 11.35 and 1, that's about 0.081.

92
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:18,296
And a quick one from practice midterm three: six training points, five regions.

93
00:06:18,296 --> 00:06:21,830
A region with one point always classifies it correctly.

94
00:06:21,830 --> 00:06:25,236
Only the region with two points could hold a mistake,

95
00:06:25,236 --> 00:06:27,228
and at worst it gets one wrong.

96
00:06:27,228 --> 00:06:31,212
So training accuracy is between five sixths and one: answer C.

97
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:33,385
To recap.

98
00:06:33,385 --> 00:06:37,805
CART picks the best cut at each node, over every variable and value,

99
00:06:37,805 --> 00:06:39,104
then prunes with cp.

100
00:06:39,104 --> 00:06:44,369
A good impurity function is zero when pure, peaks at fifty-fifty, and is concave,

101
00:06:44,369 --> 00:06:47,944
so splits never raise it, though they may not lower it.

102
00:06:47,944 --> 00:06:51,908
Go left when the condition is true, and with a missing value,

103
00:06:51,908 --> 00:06:54,443
follow every branch you can't rule out.

104
00:06:54,443 --> 00:06:56,848
A regression tree is a step function.

105
00:06:56,848 --> 00:07:00,553
Adding a split keeps R squared within cp of where it was,

106
00:07:00,553 --> 00:07:03,802
and removing a kept split drops it by at least cp.

107
00:07:03,802 --> 00:07:05,882
Pick cp by cross-validated loss.

108
00:07:05,882 --> 00:07:08,547
Next: cross-validation and the bootstrap.
